# The dry-run recordings are compared byte-for-byte against freshly generated output by
# `npm run fixtures:dry-run:check`, which CI runs on Linux while they are usually recorded on
# Windows. Without this they would be committed with CRLF, regenerate with LF, and report as
# drifted on every CI run — the same false "phase output drifted" failure that the `*.log`
# ignore rule already caused once, arriving by a different route.
test/fixtures/dry-run/*.log text eol=lf

# The JSON fixtures have exactly the same problem, arriving a third time. `save()` in
# scripts/refresh-fixtures.js writes `JSON.stringify(...) + '\n'` and compares the result to
# the file on disk, so with core.autocrlf=true a fresh clone checks them out as CRLF and
# `npm run fixtures:check` reports EVERY ONE as "vendor payload drifted" — a full screen of
# red caused by nothing but line endings. A check that cries wolf on a clean clone is worse
# than no check: it is what teaches you to stop reading the output.
test/fixtures/**/*.json text eol=lf
